### Executive Summary

This memorandum provides a detailed examination of Novus Biopharma Inc.'s ongoing initiatives in drug development, emphasizing our current clinical trials, FDA approval pathways, and manufacturing strategies. As a leader in biopharmaceutical innovation, Novus is committed to delivering transformative therapies that address unmet medical needs. Our pipeline includes promising candidates in oncology, neurology, and autoimmune disorders, with a focus on precision medicine and biologic therapies.

### 1. Introduction to Drug Development

The journey of drug development begins with the identification of viable drug candidates, followed by rigorous preclinical and clinical testing. Novus Biopharma has adopted a phased approach to drug development, leveraging cutting-edge technologies and extensive collaboration with industry leaders and academic institutions.

#### 1.1 Research and Discovery

Our research pipeline is predominantly centered around three key therapeutic areas: oncology, neurology, and autoimmune diseases. The drug discovery team, led by Dr. Mark Robinson, has made significant progress with our lead candidate, NBP-202, a novel small molecule inhibitor targeting the BRAF mutation in metastatic melanoma. Preclinical studies conducted at our state-of-the-art facilities in Cambridge, MA, demonstrated a 75% reduction in tumor volume in xenograft models.

### 2. Clinical Trials Overview

Clinical trials are essential for assessing the safety and efficacy of new drugs. Novus Biopharma adheres to rigorous standards set forth by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA). Our clinical trials are designed in compliance with Good Clinical Practice (GCP) guidelines.

#### 2.1 Phase I Trials

Phase I trials for NBP-202 commenced in January 2024 at the Massachusetts General Hospital (MGH) under the supervision of Dr. Sarah Patel, a renowned oncologist. This phase aims to evaluate the drug's safety profile and pharmacokinetics. The trial enrolled 40 healthy volunteers, with preliminary results indicating a favorable safety profile and tolerable side effects.

#### 2.2 Phase II Trials

Pending positive results from Phase I, we plan to initiate Phase II trials in July 2024. Dr. James Thompson, Director of Clinical Research at Novus, has outlined a multicenter trial strategy involving 15 leading oncology centers across the United States, including the Johns Hopkins Hospital and the University of California, San Francisco (UCSF). The objective is to assess the drug's efficacy in a patient population with confirmed BRAF mutations.

#### 2.3 Phase III Trials

Assuming successful outcomes in Phase II, we anticipate launching Phase III trials in early 2025. These trials will be pivotal in substantiating the drug's clinical benefits and will involve a larger cohort of 600 patients across multiple geographic locations, including sites in Europe and Asia.

### 3. Regulatory Pathways and FDA Approval

The regulatory process for drug approval is complex and requires careful navigation of FDA requirements. Our Regulatory Affairs team, led by Carla Sanchez, is tasked with ensuring compliance throughout the development process.

#### 3.1 FDA Submission Process

The first step in gaining FDA approval is the submission of an Investigational New Drug (IND) application. Novus submitted the IND for NBP-202 in December 2023, which included comprehensive data from preclinical studies, proposed trial protocols, and manufacturing details. The FDA granted fast-track designation for our drug, recognizing its potential to address a serious condition and fill an unmet medical need.

#### 3.2 Communication with the FDA

Communication with the FDA is ongoing, with regular meetings scheduled to discuss trial progress and any necessary adjustments. Our designated FDA liaison, Dr. Emily Rivera, has established a strong rapport with the reviewing division, facilitating a smoother review process.

### 4. Manufacturing Capabilities

Manufacturing the drug at scale requires precision and adherence to stringent quality control measures. Novus operates a cGMP (current Good Manufacturing Practice) compliant facility in Durham, NC, capable of producing both small molecules and biologics.

#### 4.1 Production Process

The production of NBP-202 involves a multi-step synthesis process, starting with the procurement of high-purity raw materials from suppliers such as ChemSupply Inc. and Sigma-Aldrich. The synthesis pathway has been optimized to enhance yield and minimize by-products. Our production team, led by Dr. Lisa Nguyen, is currently finalizing scale-up procedures to meet projected market demands.

#### 4.2 Quality Assurance

Quality assurance is paramount at every stage of manufacturing. Our Quality Control (QC) department conducts rigorous testing of raw materials, in-process samples, and final products to ensure compliance with FDA standards. We utilize advanced analytical methods, including LC-MS and HPLC, to verify the identity, potency, and purity of NBP-202.

### 5. Market Access and Commercialization Strategy

Following successful clinical trials and FDA approval, Novus Biopharma has developed a comprehensive commercialization strategy for NBP-202. Our Market Access team, led by Michael Chen, is responsible for securing reimbursement and facilitating market entry.

#### 5.1 Pricing Strategy

We are currently conducting health economic assessments to establish a competitive pricing strategy. Collaborations with external consulting firms, including IQVIA and Avalere Health, are underway to analyze market trends and payer perspectives.

#### 5.2 Distribution Channels

A strategic partnership has been formed with Cardinal Health for the distribution of NBP-202 to healthcare providers across the United States. This collaboration will ensure timely delivery and accessibility of our drug to patients once approved.

### 6. Future Directions

Moving forward, Novus Biopharma is committed to expanding its pipeline and addressing additional therapeutic areas. Ongoing research initiatives include exploring combination therapies for melanoma with our pipeline candidate NBP-203, currently in preclinical development.

#### 6.1 Investment in Innovation

To foster innovation, Novus is investing in advanced technologies such as artificial intelligence and big data analytics to enhance drug discovery and development processes. Collaborations with technology firms like IBM Watson and Tempus Labs are in progress to harness data insights for better decision-making.

#### 6.2 Global Expansion

Looking ahead, Novus is exploring opportunities for global expansion, focusing on emerging markets in Asia and Latin America. Strategic partnerships with local pharmaceutical companies, such as Sinopharm in China and Grupo Farmaceutico in Brazil, will facilitate market entry and distribution.

