### 12. Clinical Trial Transparency and Post-Marketing Studies

In addition to our robust pharmacovigilance efforts, Novus Biopharma is committed to ensuring transparency in our clinical trials and post-marketing studies for NBP-202. This commitment not only fosters public trust but also aligns with regulatory requirements and ethical standards in the pharmaceutical industry.

#### 12.1 Clinical Trial Results Disclosure

We will adhere to the principle of transparency by publicly disclosing the results of clinical trials associated with NBP-202, regardless of their outcomes. This will include the publication of data in peer-reviewed journals, registration of trials in clinical trial registries (such as ClinicalTrials.gov), and providing summaries of findings to participants and stakeholders. By sharing both positive and negative results, we aim to contribute to the broader scientific community and enhance the understanding of NBP-202's effectiveness and safety profile.

**Example:** If a trial shows that NBP-202 significantly improves overall survival rates in melanoma patients compared to standard therapies, this data will be published in a leading oncology journal. Conversely, if adverse reactions are observed, those results will also be transparently reported to inform ongoing treatment practices.

#### 12.2 Post-Marketing Surveillance Studies

To further ensure the ongoing safety and efficacy of NBP-202, we will initiate post-marketing surveillance studies. These studies will gather data on long-term outcomes and rare adverse events that may not have been evident during pre-launch clinical trials. Collaborating with academic institutions and research organizations, we will track patient outcomes and experiences over time.

**Example:** A post-marketing study may involve a cohort of patients treated with NBP-202 who will be monitored for several years to assess any late-onset side effects. This data will be critical not only in evaluating the drug's safety profile but also in identifying potential patient populations that may benefit most from the treatment.

#### 12.3 Engagement with Regulatory Authorities

To maintain compliance and ensure the highest standards of safety, we will engage regularly with regulatory authorities such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA). This engagement will include submitting periodic safety update reports (PSURs) and responding promptly to any inquiries or concerns raised by these agencies. Our dedicated regulatory affairs team will ensure that NBP-202 remains compliant with all applicable regulations and guidelines.

**Example:** Following the launch of NBP-202, any significant findings from post-marketing studies will be communicated to the FDA as part of our commitment to ongoing safety monitoring and risk management.

